{"lecture_id": "W02.L1", "title": "Model Context Protocols and Architecture", "slides": [{"kind": "concept", "title": "The Need for Model Context Protocols", "bullets": ["AI tools historically required custom, fragmented integrations for every data source and IDE.", "Model Context Protocol (MCP) standardizes how models securely access contextual data and local tools.", "Decouples AI application logic from backend server development using open protocol specifications.", "Enables plug-and-play connectivity between IDEs, desktop hosts, and specialized context servers."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "As developers integrate LLMs into software engineering workflows, connecting models to local files, databases, and APIs has traditionally required bespoke implementations. Every IDE or coding assistant built custom plugins for every tool. Model Context Protocol, or MCP, solves this fragmentation by defining a unified protocol. An AI host like an IDE can connect to any standard MCP server regardless of how that server exposes file systems, databases, or workflow pipelines.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 5}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "MCP High-Level System Architecture", "bullets": ["Architecture features three main entities: Host Application, MCP Client, and MCP Server.", "The Host is the user-facing application like an IDE or AI agent runner.", "The Client maintains 1:1 connections with servers and translates host intent into protocol messages.", "The Server exposes specific capabilities: reading resources, offering prompts, or running tools."], "figure": {"kind": "flow", "caption": "MCP Data Flow Architecture", "steps": ["Host Application (IDE)", "MCP Client Interface", "JSON-RPC Message Layer", "MCP Server", "Local System / External Tools"], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "The architecture relies on a clear separation of concerns. The Host Application provides the user interface and coordinates overall task execution. Inside or alongside the host, an MCP Client manages standard protocol connections. Each MCP Server runs independently, exposing specialized tools or contextual resources over JSON-RPC. This decoupled design ensures isolated process environments and clean boundary enforcement across local machine resources.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Client-Server Communication Model", "bullets": ["Communication occurs over bi-directional channels using JSON-RPC 2.0 formatting.", "Clients send requests or notifications to servers; servers execute commands and reply.", "Servers can also send notifications to inform clients of dynamic resource updates.", "State is managed through connection lifecycles rather than global persistent stores."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Unlike simple unidirectional REST APIs, MCP uses a bi-directional messaging model powered by JSON-RPC 2.0. This allows clients to initiate requests for tool execution or resource reading, while servers can notify clients when underlying data changes or when long-running operations progress. Understanding this stateful client-server connection is essential when auditing developer workspace configurations.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "The Three Core Primitives of MCP", "bullets": ["Resources: File-like data streams exposed via URI templates for context retrieval.", "Prompts: Reusable prompt templates and workflows provided dynamically by servers.", "Tools: Executable functions that allow AI models to perform side-effect actions.", "Each primitive follows a standardized declaration schema and execution lifecycle."], "figure": {"kind": "table", "caption": "Comparison of MCP Primitives", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": [["Primitive", "Access Pattern", "Side Effects", "Primary Purpose"], ["Resources", "Read-Only (URI)", "No", "Context Injection"], ["Prompts", "Template Invocation", "No", "Guided Workflows"], ["Tools", "Function Call", "Yes", "System Actions"]]}, "quiz": null, "notes": "MCP defines three foundational primitives. Resources act as read-only context sources identified by URI schemes like file or database handles. Prompts are predefined interaction templates that help structure multi-step development tasks. Tools are invokable functions that allow models to modify files, run tests, or execute terminal commands safely under developer authorization.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 7}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Protocol Messaging via JSON-RPC 2.0", "bullets": ["Messages must strictly adhere to the JSON-RPC 2.0 specification payload rules.", "Request objects require jsonrpc, id, method, and an optional params dictionary.", "Response objects contain matching id, plus either result or error keys.", "Notifications omit the id field and expect no response from the receiver."], "figure": {"kind": "code", "caption": "Sample JSON-RPC Request and Response", "steps": [], "code": "{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 1,
  "method": "tools/call",
  "params": {
    "name": "run_unit_tests",
    "arguments": {"test_suite": "auth_tests"}
  }
}

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 1,
  "result": {
    "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Passed 5/5 tests"}]
  }
}", "language": "json", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "JSON-RPC 2.0 is the messaging backbone of MCP. Notice that every request has a unique identifier matching its response. If a field is missing or incorrectly formatted—such as omitting the jsonrpc version string—the message will fail validation. Notifications do not include an ID because they do not wait for an explicit answer.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": [], "minutes": 7}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Transport Protocols: Stdio vs HTTP/SSE", "bullets": ["Standard Input/Output (stdio): Local server execution via subprocess pipes.", "HTTP with Server-Sent Events (SSE): Remote server communication over network sockets.", "Stdio provides strong process isolation and direct local system process management.", "SSE handles distributed agents, remote microservices, and containerized servers."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "MCP supports two standard transport layers: stdio and HTTP with Server-Sent Events (SSE). Stdio is commonly used for local command-line tools and local desktop workflows, where the host directly spawns the server binary. HTTP with SSE allows cloud-hosted or remote services to stream notifications and responses back to local developer IDEs.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 7}, {"kind": "check", "title": "Checkpoint 1: Core Primitives and Transport", "bullets": [], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": {"question": "Which MCP primitive should be configured if you want an AI agent to read context from a file URI without executing any side-effecting commands?", "options": ["Tools, because tools return file data to the prompt context.", "Resources, because resources provide read-only context retrieval without side effects.", "Prompts, because prompts automatically fetch local file content on launch.", "Transports, because transport channels manage file read streams."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Option 1 is correct because Resources are designed specifically for read-only context data identified by URIs without triggering side-effect actions. Option 0 is incorrect because tools execute actions and produce side effects. Option 2 is incorrect because prompts are workflow templates, not resource readers."}, "notes": "Let's review our understanding of MCP primitives. Remember that resources are read-only contextual streams, while tools execute side effects. Identifying the proper primitive prevents configuration errors and unwanted execution risks in developer workspace setups.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": [], "minutes": 5}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Capability Negotiation and Initialization Handshake", "bullets": ["Connection begins with an explicit initialize request sent from Client to Server.", "Handshake exchanges client/server protocol versions and supported capabilities.", "Client capabilities include roots handling and sampling assistance.", "Server capabilities declare resource subscription support, tools, and prompts."], "figure": {"kind": "flow", "caption": "Initialization Sequence", "steps": ["Client sends initialize request", "Server evaluates capabilities", "Server responds with protocol version", "Client sends initialized notification", "Active Session Established"], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Before exchanging resources or calling tools, the client and server undergo an initialization handshake. The client sends an 'initialize' request containing supported protocol versions and client features. The server replies with its operational capabilities. Once the client returns an 'initialized' notification, protocol channels are active.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": [], "minutes": 7}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Workspace Configuration File Structure", "bullets": ["Workspace templates define MCP server declarations in a structured JSON file (e.g., mcp.json).", "Root key mcpServers maps unique server identifiers to execution specifications.", "Each server entry requires command or url depending on transport type (stdio vs sse).", "Key fields include command, args, env, and disabled flags."], "figure": {"kind": "code", "caption": "Basic MCP Workspace Configuration", "steps": [], "code": "{
  "mcpServers": {
    "filesystem": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem", "/workspace"],
      "env": {
        "DEBUG": "true"
      }
    }
  }
}", "language": "json", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "In developer workspace templates, MCP integrations are configured through JSON files like mcp.json or within IDE settings. The configuration maps a server name to its execution profile. For stdio servers, you must supply the command executable, string array arguments, and optional environment variables.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": [], "minutes": 7}, {"kind": "example", "title": "Configuring Dual Transports in Workspace Schema", "bullets": ["Demonstrates defining both local stdio and remote sse servers in one schema file.", "command specifies executable path for local processes.", "url defines HTTP endpoint for remote connection channels.", "Schema validators check for required fields based on the configured transport mode."], "figure": {"kind": "code", "caption": "Multi-Server Workspace Configuration Schema", "steps": [], "code": "{
  "mcpServers": {
    "local-db": {
      "command": "python",
      "args": ["-m", "db_mcp_server"],
      "env": {"DB_PATH": "./data/app.db"}
    },
    "cloud-metrics": {
      "url": "https://api.internal.net/mcp/sse",
      "headers": {"Authorization": "Bearer token123"}
    }
  }
}", "language": "json", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Here is a standard workspace configuration declaring two servers. The first server uses stdio with local Python binaries, passing environment variables like DB_PATH. The second uses an HTTP SSE connection, requiring a URL and request headers. Workspace validation tools inspect this configuration to verify field types and schema compliance.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": [], "minutes": 8}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Resource URIs and Context Hydration", "bullets": ["Resources use standardized URIs (e.g., postgres://database/schema, file:///logs/app.log).", "Resource templates allow parameterized queries (e.g., file:///{path}).", "The host reads resource contents using resources/read calls during context construction.", "Enables dynamic context hydration without baking static strings into system prompts."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Resource URIs follow standard URI syntax. Server schemas define parameterized templates, allowing AI agents to dynamically fetch relevant logs, database schemas, or API definitions when formulating responses. This process, known as context hydration, delivers accurate context without bloating prompts.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Tool Schema Definitions via JSON Schema", "bullets": ["Server tools declare parameters using standard JSON Schema definitions.", "Required fields, argument types, and descriptions guide LLM tool choice and formatting.", "The host inspects schema before authorizing tool calls.", "Schema mismatch errors cause protocol validation failures prior to execution."], "figure": {"kind": "code", "caption": "MCP Tool Declaration Object Schema", "steps": [], "code": "{
  "name": "git_commit",
  "description": "Commits staged changes to git repository",
  "inputSchema": {
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
      "message": {"type": "string", "description": "Commit message"}
    },
    "required": ["message"]
  }
}", "language": "json", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Tool definitions rely heavily on standard JSON Schema syntax inside inputSchema. The host application presents this schema to the LLM so it knows what parameters are required. If a tool call omits a required property like 'message', the workspace validator or client runtime rejects the request immediately.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": [], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Error Codes and Protocol Validation Requirements", "bullets": ["Standard JSON-RPC error codes must be returned on invalid messages.", "-32600: Invalid Request (malformed JSON structure or missing fields).", "-32601: Method Not Found (server does not support the requested operation).", "-32602: Invalid Params (tool arguments fail JSON Schema validation)."], "figure": {"kind": "table", "caption": "Standard Protocol Error Codes", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": [["Error Code", "Name", "Trigger Condition"], ["-32700", "Parse Error", "Invalid JSON payload sent"], ["-32600", "Invalid Request", "Schema missing required RPC fields"], ["-32601", "Method Not Found", "Unsupported MCP capability method"], ["-32602", "Invalid Params", "Arguments violate tool input schema"]]}, "quiz": null, "notes": "When reviewing workspace configurations or debugging tool calls, knowledge of JSON-RPC error codes is critical. Error -32600 indicates structural config problems in requests, while -32602 indicates that the arguments supplied by an agent violated the tool's JSON Schema parameter rules.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": [], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "check", "title": "Checkpoint 2: Configuration and Protocol Schema", "bullets": [], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": {"question": "A developer inspects a workspace mcp.json file and notices that an args parameter inside a stdio server entry is set to "args": "--port 8080" instead of an array. What issue will occur?", "options": ["The client will silently convert the string into a list and execute successfully.", "A schema validation error will trigger because args must be an array of strings in standard MCP schema.", "The server will execute over HTTP SSE instead of standard input/output.", "The host will ignore the argument and pass default environment parameters."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Option 1 is correct because the MCP workspace schema specifies that args for a stdio server process must be an array of string tokens, not a single string. Option 0 is incorrect because rigid protocol schema validation prevents implicit string splitting."}, "notes": "Let's check our understanding of configuration schemas. Remember that schema validators check key data types strictly. In MCP configurations for stdio, args must always be an array of strings.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": [], "minutes": 5}, {"kind": "example", "title": "Analyzing a Complete Workspace Template", "bullets": ["Highlighting real-world workspace template requirements and configuration structure.", "Shows environment variable expansion and explicit argument formatting.", "Verifies server capability requirements and process isolation settings.", "Serves as the benchmark pattern for environment configuration tests."], "figure": {"kind": "code", "caption": "Complete Production-Ready MCP Config", "steps": [], "code": "{
