{"lecture_id": "W02.L1", "title": "Model Context Protocols and Architecture", "slides": [{"kind": "concept", "title": "Introduction to Model Context Protocols", "bullets": ["Model Context Protocol (MCP) standardizes how AI models access external systems and context.", "Eliminates custom one-off integration glue code for every developer tool and repository.", "Enables seamless discovery of local tools, file systems, and external service APIs."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Welcome to week 2. In our previous week, we explored general developer workflows with AI assistance. Today, we delve into Model Context Protocols, an open architecture that allows language models to interface safely and consistently with workspace tools, databases, and services. MCP standardizes context ingestion so developers do not need bespoke integration code for every single tool.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 7}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Why Standardization of Context Matters", "bullets": ["Non-standardized context leads to fragmented tool APIs and fragile prompt engineering.", "Standardized schemas ensure consistent tool signatures and reliable response handling.", "Uniform protocols decouple the AI client from underlying language runtimes and environments."], "figure": {"kind": "table", "caption": "Custom Integration vs Standardized MCP Architecture", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": [["Aspect", "Custom Integrations", "MCP Architecture"], ["Tool Schema", "Ad-hoc JSON or string prompts", "Standard JSON Schema specifications"], ["Transport", "Varies per plugin system", "Standard JSON-RPC over stdio or HTTP"], ["Scalability", "High maintenance overhead", "Reusable servers across multiple IDEs"]]}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Before standardized protocols, connecting an LLM to local file systems or developer tools required custom extensions for each editor or framework. MCP provides a universal interface. Like Language Server Protocol revolutionized code editors, MCP standardizes how AI models read resources, invoke tools, and retrieve context prompts across diverse developer environments.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 7}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "High-Level Architecture of Model Context Protocol", "bullets": ["Follows a clean client-server architecture inside the developer workspace.", "Hosts encapsulate the AI application such as an IDE or CLI tool.", "Clients maintain isolated connections with MCP servers handling specific domains."], "figure": {"kind": "flow", "caption": "MCP Architecture Data Flow", "steps": ["Host / AI Application", "MCP Client", "Transport Layer (stdio/HTTP)", "MCP Server", "External Resource / Tool"], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "The architecture separates concerns into Host, Client, Server, and Resources. The Host is the top-level application, such as VS Code or an AI CLI. The Client lives inside the Host and manages communication. The MCP Server exposes specific capabilities—like reading git history or running unit tests—via standardized protocol messages.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 7}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "MCP Host, Client, and Server Roles", "bullets": ["MCP Host controls application UI, user permissions, and AI orchestration.", "MCP Client initiates protocol handshakes and routes requests to designated servers.", "MCP Server implements capabilities like resources, prompts, and executable tools."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Understanding these roles is crucial for workspace configuration. The MCP Host acts as the container. It spawns MCP Clients, which establish connections to individual MCP Servers. Each server operates independently in its own process, exposing resources or executable functions while adhering to strict context protocol rules.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 7}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Communication Transports: Stdio vs WebSockets and HTTP", "bullets": ["Stdio transport launches local server subprocesses via standard input and output channels.", "HTTP with Server-Sent Events connects remote or containerized MCP servers.", "Both transports serialize protocol messages using standard JSON-RPC 2.0 format."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "MCP supports two primary transport mechanisms. Local workspace setups typically use stdio, where the client spawns the server binary directly as a subprocess and communicates over standard input and output. For remote or cloud-hosted tools, HTTP with Server-Sent Events or WebSockets is used. Regardless of transport, the payload structure remains identical JSON-RPC 2.0.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 7}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Core MCP Schema: Server Capabilities", "bullets": ["Servers declare supported features during initial handshake capability negotiation.", "Main capability primitives include resources, prompts, and tools.", "Capability declarations prevent clients from sending unsupported requests to servers."], "figure": {"kind": "code", "caption": "MCP Handshake Server Capabilities Declaration", "steps": [], "code": "{
