{"lecture_id": "W02.L1", "title": "Model Context Protocols and Architecture", "slides": [{"kind": "concept", "title": "Introduction to Model Context Protocols (MCP)", "bullets": ["Connects Large Language Models seamlessly to local tools, databases, and developer environment contexts.", "Standardizes how applications provide structured context and executable capabilities to AI models.", "Eliminates custom one-off integrations between individual development tools and AI coding assistants.", "Enables secure, modular extensibility for developer environments and automated workflows."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Model Context Protocol (MCP) provides an open standard for AI models to interact with external tools and context sources. Before MCP, connecting an AI assistant to a local database, git repository, or specialized compiler required custom integration code for every client. MCP decouples the host environment from the context providers by defining a universal client-server interaction protocol.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "The Client-Server Architecture of MCP", "bullets": ["Host applications act as MCP clients or contain embedded MCP clients.", "MCP servers expose specialized data sources, prompt templates, and executable tools.", "Protocol decouples LLM interface logic from underlying system implementation details.", "Multi-server support allows clients to aggregate capabilities from diverse domain tools simultaneously."], "figure": {"kind": "flow", "caption": "High-level MCP architecture showing decoupling between host application and external tools.", "steps": ["Host App (Client)", "MCP Protocol", "MCP Server (Tools/Resources)", "External System"], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "In an MCP architecture, the host application (such as an IDE or command-line coding agent) manages the user interaction and LLM queries while acting as the MCP client. External processes run as MCP servers. A host can connect to multiple MCP servers concurrently, creating a unified context fabric without coupling tool logic directly into the IDE.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Core Roles: Host, Client, and Server", "bullets": ["Host Application: The top-level software coordinating overall LLM workflows and user interactions.", "MCP Client: Maintains 1:1 connections with servers and forwards request-response payloads.", "MCP Server: Provides contextual primitives (Tools, Resources, Prompts) via standard endpoints.", "LLM Integration: The client formats server capabilities into model context or function calls."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Understanding the distinction between Host, Client, and Server is critical. The host application provides the user interface and coordinates overall task execution. Inside or alongside the host, an MCP client manages individual connections to external MCP servers. Servers are lightweight background processes that expose capabilities without needing knowledge of which specific LLM is driving the request.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Communication Transports: Stdio and HTTP+SSE", "bullets": ["Standard Input/Output (stdio): Used for local processes spawned directly by the host client.", "HTTP with Server-Sent Events (SSE): Used for remote or network-isolated server deployments.", "Stdio provides low-latency, secure local communication without network port allocation.", "HTTP+SSE enables remote server streaming and persistent full-duplex client-server message flow."], "figure": {"kind": "table", "caption": "Comparison of standard MCP transport layers.", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": [["Transport", "Use Case", "Communication Path", "Security Model"], ["stdio", "Local sub-process", "Standard in/out streams", "Process isolation"], ["HTTP + SSE", "Remote / Networked", "POST requests + SSE stream", "Network auth / TLS"]]}, "quiz": null, "notes": "MCP standardizes two transport mechanisms: stdio and HTTP with Server-Sent Events (SSE). Stdio is ideal for local developer tools where the host spawns the server process directly, avoiding firewall prompts and port conflicts. HTTP with Server-Sent Events (SSE) is used for remote servers where the server streams updates over SSE and receives client messages over HTTP POST. WebSockets are not part of the standard MCP specification.", "los": ["CO1.LO1"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 7}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "JSON-RPC 2.0 Message Structure", "bullets": ["All communication relies on lightweight JSON-RPC 2.0 messages over the active transport stream.", "Requests require unique id, method name, and optional structured params object.", "Responses return matching id with either result payload or structured error object.", "Notifications transmit unidirectional events without expecting a response payload or message ID."], "figure": {"kind": "code", "caption": "JSON-RPC 2.0 request example for invoking an MCP tool.", "steps": [], "code": "{
