}", "language": "json", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Here is a concrete JSON schema for a tool named run_linter. Notice how required attributes are explicitly listed and enum values restrict acceptable choices. Providing clear parameter descriptions prevents the model from generating malformed inputs.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 7}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Implementing the MCP Server Event Loop", "bullets": ["Initialize the server instance using standard SDK entry points.", "Register request handlers for ListTools and CallTool capabilities.", "Format return payloads into structured text or binary content objects."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "On the implementation side, an MCP server listens for client requests. When the client issues a ListTools request, the server returns all declared tools and schemas. When CallTool is received, the server executes the corresponding logic and returns structured text or binary response content.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "worked_problem", "title": "Worked Problem: Building a Local Test Execution Tool", "bullets": ["Step 1: Instantiate the MCP Server with explicit tool capabilities.", "Step 2: Declare the schema for executing JUnit test suites.", "Step 3: Route incoming call requests to execute local shell processes."], "figure": {"kind": "code", "caption": "TypeScript Implementation of a Custom MCP Tool Server", "steps": [], "code": "import { Server } from '@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/index.js';
import { StdioServerTransport } from '@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/stdio.js';
import { CallToolRequestSchema, ListToolsRequestSchema } from '@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/types.js';

const server = new Server({ name: 'test-runner', version: '1.0.0' }, { capabilities: { tools: {} } });

server.setRequestHandler(ListToolsRequestSchema, async () => ({
  tools: [{ name: 'run_tests', description: 'Run test suite', inputSchema: { type: 'object', properties: {} } }]
}));

server.setRequestHandler(CallToolRequestSchema, async (req) => {
  if (req.params.name === 'run_tests') {
    return { content: [{ type: 'text', text: 'All tests passed (12/12)' }] };
  }
  throw new Error('Tool not found');
});", "language": "typescript", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "In this worked problem, we see a complete server handler written in TypeScript. Notice how the request handlers respond to tool discovery (ListTools) and execution (CallTool). Ensuring correct handler typing prevents runtime errors and static analyzer warnings.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 10}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Workspace Configuration & Environment Variables", "bullets": ["Agents load tools from workspace configuration files like mcp_config.json.", "Config specifies binary command, execution arguments, and environment variables.", "Proper path resolution and variable passing ensure portable tool execution."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Once an MCP server is written, the agent workspace host must be configured to launch it. Workspace configuration files map server keys to execution commands (such as node or python binaries) and environment variables needed for external tools.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "check", "title": "Validating Integration Configurations", "bullets": [], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": {"question": "Why is explicit JSON Schema type definition critical when configuring custom MCP tools for an agent workspace?", "options": ["It increases network transfer speeds over standard I/O.", "It allows the agent client to validate arguments and prevents malformed model requests.", "It eliminates the need to compile TypeScript code before server startup.", "It bypasses operating system environment variable restrictions."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Explicit JSON schema definitions allow the client and server to perform static and runtime type verification on function arguments. Option 0 is wrong because schemas define data structure, not network hardware bandwidth."}, "notes": "Consider the role schema validation plays between the model generating raw JSON and the tool receiving binary execution parameters.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 4}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Static Analysis and Schema Integrity", "bullets": ["Custom server configurations must pass strict static analysis without warnings.", "Mismatches between JSON Schema definitions and runtime handler types cause failure.", "Ensuring type safety prevents runtime deserialization bugs during agent workflows."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Configuring custom tools is not just about writing running code; your code and schemas must meet strict production quality checks. Static analysis tools verify that parameters passed to and from tool handlers conform to strict type bounds without type coercion warnings.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "worked_problem", "title": "Worked Problem: Fixing Schema Validation & Warning Errors", "bullets": ["Identify issue: Parameter type ambiguity causing static analysis warning.", "Refactor schema: Add strict property constraints and explicit required fields.", "Verify: Run static analysis to confirm zero reported warnings and passing integration tests."], "figure": {"kind": "code", "caption": "Refactoring Schema to Eliminate Warnings", "steps": [], "code": "// Invalid / Warning-prone Schema
const looseSchema = { type: "object", properties: { timeout: { type: "any" } } };

// Corrected / Clean Schema passing static analysis
const strictSchema = {
  type: "object",
  properties: {
    timeoutSeconds: { type: "integer", minimum: 1, maximum: 300 }
  },
  required: ["timeoutSeconds"],
  additionalProperties: false
};", "language": "json", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "In this worked problem, we fix a common issue where loose type definitions trigger static analyzer warnings. Changing 'any' types to strictly bounded primitives (e.g., integer with min/max) ensures type safety, silences static analysis warnings, and guarantees deterministic behavior.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 9}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Testing MCP Tool Integrations", "bullets": ["Unit test individual tool execution handlers in isolation.", "Mock stdio/SSE client connections for end-to-end integration tests.", "Validate that public test suites pass completely prior to deploying workspace tools."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Automated testing of tool integrations requires two phases: unit testing the tool's underlying business logic, and integration testing the protocol handshake. Standard integration tests spin up the server over stdio, send protocol JSON-RPC messages, and assert schema conformance.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "activity", "title": "Hands-on Workspace Tool Integration", "bullets": ["Task: Create and configure a custom tool server for running a linter.", "Step 1: Write an MCP server exposing a `run_linter` tool.", "Step 2: Add server configuration to your workspace `mcp_config.json`.", "Step 3: Run static analyzer and integration tests to ensure 100% pass rate with 0 warnings."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Now open your local dev machine environment for Lab 3. Your goal is to construct a custom MCP server, register it within your agent workspace config, run static analysis to confirm zero warnings, and pass all public integration test suites.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 15}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Query Tuning Frameworks for MCP Workflows", "bullets": ["Large tool execution outputs can overwhelm model context windows.", "Query tuning frameworks filter, truncate, and format tool payload results.", "Structured output filtering improves downstream reasoning and reduces token overhead."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "When running external tools like test runners or compilers, tool outputs can be thousands of lines long. As highlighted in recent literature (C1), applying query tuning frameworks to tool payload streams filters redundant log text, keeping model context clean and optimizing workspace workflow efficiency.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 7}, {"kind": "summary", "title": "Summary: Custom Tool Integrations with MCP", "bullets": ["MCP provides a standardized client-server protocol for extending AI agents with tools.", "Tools require explicit JSON Schemas, robust request handlers, and workspace config bindings.", "High-quality tool integration requires passing static analysis without warnings and green public test suites."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "To summarize, Model Context Protocol provides the robust foundation needed to plug real-world software development tools into agent workspaces. By adhering to strict JSON schemas, writing clean tool handlers, and tuning context outputs, we build reliable, automated software engineering workflows.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 5}]}

{"lecture_id": "W03.L2", "title": "Custom Tool Integrations Using Model Context Protocols", "slides": [{"kind": "concept", "title": "What is the Model Context Protocol?", "bullets": ["Open standard enabling AI agents to interact with external tools and data sources securely.", "Decouples host development environments from tool-specific execution logic and runtime drivers.", "Replaces ad-hoc, proprietary function-calling glue code with standardized interface protocols.", "Facilitates seamless integration of linters, build systems, database connectors, and custom APIs."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Welcome everyone. Today we delve into custom tool integrations using the Model Context Protocol, commonly abbreviated as MCP. As AI coding agents become central to modern software workflows, standardizing how an agent discovers and executes developer tools is critical. MCP provides a universal specification for agents to interact with localized or remote capabilities without writing custom integration code for every tool.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Core MCP Architecture: Client, Host, and Server", "bullets": ["Host application manages agent context, user interface sessions, and model interactions.", "MCP Client embedded within host establishes standard protocol connection to tools.", "MCP Server exposes executable tools, state resources, and workflow prompt templates.", "Communication flows bidirectionally over standard IO (stdio) or Server-Sent Events (SSE)."], "figure": {"kind": "flow", "caption": "MCP System Architecture and Message Routing", "steps": ["Agent Host & Client", "Transport Layer (stdio/SSE)", "MCP Tool Server", "External Developer Environment"], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "To understand custom integration, we must look at the three primary architecture layers: Host, Client, and Server. The Host is the IDE or agent workspace. Inside the Host sits the MCP Client. The Client talks over a transport stream—typically stdio or SSE—to an MCP Server. The Server wraps external developer tools like JUnit or static code analyzers and presents them in a standard protocol format.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": ["C1"], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Tool Declarations and JSON Schemas", "bullets": ["MCP tools require unique identifiers, human-readable descriptions, and input parameters.", "Input parameter formats strictly adhere to standard JSON Schema specifications.", "Detailed descriptions guide the LLM on when and how to invoke the tool effectively.", "Strict schema definitions prevent runtime payload parsing errors during execution."], "figure": {"kind": "code", "caption": "Example Tool Definition Schema in JSON", "steps": [], "code": "{
  "name": "run_linter",
  "description": "Runs static code analysis on specified project directory",
  "inputSchema": {
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
      "target_dir": { "type": "string" }
    },
    "required": ["target_dir"]
  }
}", "language": "json", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Every tool exposed by an MCP server must be formally declared using a JSON Schema. The description field is particularly vital because the agent's underlying LLM uses this text to decide when to call the tool. The inputSchema defines exact expected types and required parameters, enforcing structural correctness before execution.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Standard MCP Message Exchange Lifecycle", "bullets": ["Client sends initialize request to negotiate protocol versions and capability flags.", "Client discovers available capabilities via tools/list request payload.", "Agent constructs tools/call request with parameters validated against tool schema.", "Server executes target execution logic and returns structured content response."], "figure": {"kind": "flow", "caption": "Protocol Message Exchange Sequence", "steps": ["Initialize Protocol Handshake", "Tool Discovery (tools/list)", "Tool Dispatch (tools/call)", "Payload Response"], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Let me walk you through the protocol message lifecycle. When the agent workspace starts up, the Client performs an initial handshake to verify compatibility. Next, it queries tools/list to index all tools registered by the Server. When the user requests an action, the agent formats a tools/call request, and the server executes the tool, returning structured text or image payloads.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "concept", "title": "Implementing MCP Tool Server Interfaces", "bullets": ["Server frameworks provide decorators or handlers to bind code logic to protocol tools.", "Handler functions automatically validate incoming parameters against JSON Schemas.", "Responses encapsulate execution logs, status flags, and error diagnostics.", "Static code analysis checks handler signatures against protocol type definitions."], "figure": {"kind": "none", "caption": "", "steps": [], "code": "", "language": "", "rows": []}, "quiz": null, "notes": "Implementing an MCP server involves binding programming language logic to named tool interfaces. Most SDKs provide decorators to register functions easily. The SDK takes care of parsing JSON-RPC messages and validating payload arguments against the declared schema before invoking your handler.", "los": ["CO1.LO2"], "sources": [], "minutes": 6}, {"kind": "example", "title": "Constructing a Linter Tool Server", "bullets": ["Constructs an MCP server process delivering custom static code analysis capabilities.", "Decorator registers function name and auto-generates underlying JSON Schema.", "Captures execution exit codes and formats stdout/stderr into standard responses.", "Guarantees runtime error handling when requested source paths do not exist."], "figure": {"kind": "code", "caption": "Python MCP Tool Implementation", "steps": [], "code": "@mcp.tool()
def run_static_analysis(file_path: str) -> str:
    """Run static analysis check on target file path."""
    if not os.path.exists(file_path):
